<h2 id="section-1">Identify both ends of the connection</h2>
<p>Write down the reference of the base you actually own. Include the collection or version when known. A product name shared across several years can conceal a change in parts; equally, a changed name does not prove incompatibility. The useful evidence is an explicit statement about the references involved. Keep the original purchase information if it helps identify the piece, but avoid sending unrelated personal information with a product question.</p>
<p>Then identify what the new item is. “Charm” can describe a complete pendant, a decorative element without its connector, or an ornament intended for a particular base. A photograph showing an assembled piece may include components sold separately. Ask the seller to list what arrives in the box and what you need to supply. A small addition becomes less convenient if it requires an unplanned chain, connector or second base.</p>
<h2 id="section-4">Check four kinds of fit</h2>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Evidence worth asking for</th><th scope="col">Reason to pause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface</td><td>Exact compatible references and connection instructions</td><td>Only “standard fit” without a defined standard.</td></tr><tr><td>Assembly</td><td>All required components, supplied or separate</td><td>A photograph includes an unidentified adapter.</td></tr><tr><td>Orientation</td><td>Front, back and worn or movement views</td><td>The ornament faces sideways in its intended position.</td></tr><tr><td>Intended use</td><td>Confirmation for the particular wearing position</td><td>“It passes through” is the only explanation.</td></tr><tr><td>Ownership</td><td>Availability and identification of replacement components</td><td>A generic replacement is suggested without matching details.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>These checks describe different issues. Physical passage does not establish that a closure can engage normally. Engagement does not establish that the ornament faces the way you expect. A pleasing front view does not establish suitability for a different wearing position. You are looking for a supported combination, not merely a connection that can be made.</p>
<h2 id="section-7">Use a message that is easy to answer</h2>
<p>Try: “I own base reference A, purchased in this version. I would like ornament B. Can you confirm this exact combination, identify any additional part and show its normal orientation?” Add a clear photograph if the base is difficult to identify. Ask for a reference-based answer rather than a general assurance that everything in a collection works together.</p>
<p>For SUIJI, keep the base, decorative element and Core-Lock information separate. A Core-Lock tag identifies a product attribute; it is not a promise that every tagged or untagged item fits every other item. Some products use the clasp structure and some do not. Read the particular product’s contents and connection information, and ask about missing details before choosing an additional element.</p>
<h2 id="section-10">A useful comparison and a useful refusal</h2>
<p>Imagine two ornaments with a similar visible opening. One listing identifies your base and shows the assembly. The other gives an opening measurement but no intended-use information. The first provides a stronger answer to your task even if the second looks equally plausible. This does not establish which product is better overall; it establishes which combination has been explained.</p>
<p>Do not enlarge an opening, bend a fitting or force a latch to make an uncertain combination work. A jeweler may assess a modification as a separate project, with its own risks and cost. That is different from buying an interchangeable element intended to attach as supplied. If a seller cannot confirm the combination, choosing a documented alternative is a complete and sensible decision.</p>
<h2 id="section-13">Supporting checks for this decision</h2>
<p>The examples below are comparison tools, not SUIJI product prices, certifications or test results. Apply the questions to the actual reference you are considering.</p>
<h3 id="section-15">Cross-check the page, basket and terms</h3>
<p><strong>Essential facts should remain consistent throughout checkout.</strong> Confirm that quantity, variant, price, preparation time, delivery and returns do not change silently between the product page, basket and terms.</p>
<p>A page may say “sold as a pair” while the variant selector says “single”. A banner may promise delivery in 48 hours while the terms add several preparation days. “Free returns” may apply only in one country or exclude unsealed earrings. These details determine what you receive and which remedy is available.</p>
<p>Before paying, save three records: the product page with the selected variant, the basket showing the total price, and the return page. Keep the order confirmation as well. These records do not replace consumer law, but they show what was presented at the time of purchase.</p>
<h3 id="section-19">Ask a question that produces usable evidence</h3>
<p><strong>“Is it good quality?” invites a marketing answer; component questions create a record.</strong> A practical message is:</p>
<blockquote>Before I order [reference and variant], please confirm the material of the post, back and body, the colour process, weight of one earring, and which components are covered by the cited test or certificate. Please also confirm whether the price is for one piece or a pair and whether opening the package affects return eligibility.</blockquote>
<p>Save the reply. If the answer only repeats “hypoallergenic” or “meets standards”, ask once more for the component and evidence scope. Continued refusal or contradiction is a comparison signal.</p>
<h3 id="section-23">Verify every contact component</h3>
<p><strong>The decorative front may not be the material that remains against your ear.</strong> A useful listing separates post, back, hoop, solder, body, decoration and coating.</p>
<p>If a seller says “titanium earrings”, ask whether that covers both the post and back. If a certificate is offered, identify the batch and component. If the colour is PVD or plating, ask which zones are coated and what base lies underneath.</p>
<p>For sensitive ears, a precise component map is more informative than a universal “hypoallergenic” promise. For an initial piercing, online product copy cannot replace professional selection, fitting and sterility.</p>
<h3 id="section-27">Compare the total price, not the hero price</h3>
<p><strong>The meaningful figure is the amount payable for the exact contents delivered.</strong> Check whether the listing is for one earring or a pair, whether backs are included, taxes, delivery charges and any unavoidable fee.</p>
<p>Do not divide or double prices without confirming quantity. Asymmetrical sets may contain two different pieces, while piercing jewellery is often sold singly. A low unit price can become higher than a pair once two pieces and shipping are added.</p>
<h3 id="section-30">Inspect before unsealing or wearing</h3>
<p><strong>Hygiene-related return limits make the first visual check important.</strong> Compare the reference, quantity, colour, dimensions and markings with the order before opening a seal where one exists.</p>
<p>Inspect posts, backs, surfaces, pair symmetry and closure action. Photograph a scratch, missing part or faulty mechanism immediately and contact the seller before wear. Keep labels and batch references for any material claim.</p>
<p>If the product differs from the description, state the discrepancy factually. Do not wait for discomfort to report a visibly rough post or closure that fails to engage.</p>
<h3 id="section-34">Keep the evidence after delivery</h3>
<p><strong>Retain the invoice, product description and seller’s written answers for the life of the warranty or claim.</strong> If the page changes, your saved copy shows the advertised quantity, materials and finish.</p>
<p>For a reaction, the record can provide a clinician with the claimed composition. For a product defect, it helps separate a rough post, faulty closure or wrong item from a medical issue that customer service should not diagnose.</p>
<h2 id="section-37">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="section-38">Is the opening diameter enough?</h3>
<p>No. It can be one necessary measurement, but the shape of the connection, engagement, orientation and intended use still matter. Ask for the complete assembly information.</p>
<h3 id="section-40">Does the same brand guarantee compatibility?</h3>
<p>Do not assume it. Check the references or the maker’s current compatibility statement. A brand can have several systems, collections or versions serving different purposes.</p>
<h3 id="section-42">Can a charm from a bracelet become an earring?</h3>
<p>Only if the particular combination is intended and confirmed. Weight, orientation and the complete ear fitting need consideration. A successful bracelet arrangement does not establish an earring arrangement.</p>
<h3 id="section-44">What should I keep after buying?</h3>
<p>Keep the product reference, component list and compatibility response. Those details are more useful for a later addition than a photograph with no scale or identification.</p>
